[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I investigated the eBGP control plane between router1 and router2 and the data path between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2). The BGP subsystem is healthy: both routers are in Established state with default timers (keepalive 60s/hold 180s), exchanging one IPv4-unicast prefix each. Evidence: “show bgp summary” on both sides shows Established, zero drops, and one received/sent prefix; “show ip route” on router1 has B>* 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2 installed; the reverse is true on router2. FRR logs show normal keepalives and End-of-RIB with no session resets; the only “can’t get remote address” messages were at daemon startup. Data plane checks are clean: repeated pings pc1->pc2 and router1->pc2 show 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs, and all involved interfaces are up with link detected.

Mechanism: If the partner’s route were flapping, we would see BGP neighbor resets or withdrawals in the logs and route age repeatedly resetting in the RIB; none of that occurs. Localization: router1<->router2 over eth0 is stable; host-facing eth1 links are up on both routers. Root cause: No current route instability was found; prior transient messages at FRR start could have caused a brief withdrawal, but there is no ongoing issue.

Corrective fix: If you want the partner’s route “pinned” even during a BGP blip, add a floating static on router1 (e.g., ip route 200.1.1.0/24 193.10.11.2 200) so BGP remains primary and the static appears only if BGP is lost; optionally enable BFD on the eBGP session for faster failure detection.
=== END ===